p.src:
In compact notation, demo-first selling starts with the seller’s inventory: $S_1,S_2,S_3,\ldots
\quad\text{before}\quad
N \text{ is known},$ where $N$ is the buyer’s need. The seller is hoping that some $S_i$ will accidentally match a hidden problem. That is why the lecture’s critique is not merely stylistic. It is structural: $\text{show everything}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{hope something sticks}
\;\longrightarrow\;
\text{weak diagnosis}.$

